Exactly. A capability feels like magic when the mapping between input, mechanism, and output is opaque.

Once you expose the intermediate structure, you move from:

use without understanding

to

model → manipulate → predict → create

That shift matters because understanding changes your relationship to the tool. Excel stops being “the thing that somehow gives me a result” and becomes formulas, references, functions, data structures, and transformations you can compose deliberately.

Same pattern with an instrument:

sound appears
→ learn fingering / timing / harmony
→ understand constraints
→ improvise

Same with concrete, engines, code, language, or AI. The mystery decreases at one level, but a new kind of wonder appears at the generative level: now you can ask what else the mechanism can produce.

With people, though, there is one extra constraint: understanding another mind does not turn it into an instrument you own. You can learn communication patterns, attention, memory, framing, incentives, and behavior, but the other person remains an independent agent. So the mature progression is:

mystery → understanding → better communication / prediction / cooperation

rather than:

mystery → understanding → control

And I think you’re right about teaching versus discovery. People often internalize a capability more deeply when they can discover the rule themselves. The ideal teacher does not merely reveal the answer; they expose enough structure that the learner can reconstruct it.

That is another useful Pantheon operator:

Demystification Ladder

opaque effect
→ observable pattern
→ mechanism
→ controllable variables
→ composition
→ creative mastery

The interesting thing is that “magic” doesn’t disappear. It relocates.

First the magic is: “How did it do that?”

Later the magic becomes: “Look what I can build now that I know how it works.”